&lt;section id="generating-random-samples-around-rho_0" class="level1"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Generating random samples around &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho_0\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What are the different approach to generate random samples?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Generate MVN data based on &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho\)&lt;/span&gt; and compute its correlation vector &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho^{\prime}\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Generate &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho^{\prime}\)&lt;/span&gt; from &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho\)&lt;/span&gt; directly: sample randomly from the full distributional spread around &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho_0\)&lt;/span&gt; (wald-cloud):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho_0 = vech(R_0)\)&lt;/span&gt;. For testing &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(H_0: \rho = \rho_0\)&lt;/span&gt;, based on asymptotic normality of &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho\)&lt;/span&gt;, we have wald-type statistic (same as previously mentioned)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math display"&gt;\[
T = n (\hat\rho - \rho_0)^T \Psi(\rho_0)^{-1} (\hat\rho - \rho_0) \sim \chi^2_p
\]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This implies we can draw &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho\)&lt;/span&gt; under the null distribution &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(H_0\)&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math display"&gt;\[
\hat\rho^{(b)} \sim N_p(\rho_0, \frac{\Psi(\rho_0)}{n})
\]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Equivalently, the linear algebra approach:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho^{(b)} = \rho_0 + Lz\)&lt;/span&gt;, where &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(z \sim N(0,I_p)\)&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(L= \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}UD^\frac{1}{2}\)&lt;/span&gt; (eigen-decomposition of &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\Psi(\rho_0)\)&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(U\)&lt;/span&gt; is the eigenvectors , &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(D\)&lt;/span&gt; is the eigenvalue matrix)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This should give us below statement when &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(n\)&lt;/span&gt; is infinite, &lt;span class="math display"&gt;\[
Pr_{\rho_0} (\rho \in C_{1-\alpha}(\hat\rho)) = 1-\alpha
\]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Why don’t we use the first approach (Generate data then compute correlation) ?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Making extra assumption: This approach assumes the underlying data distribution, which seems to be unnecessary here in our theory. We only need &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho\)&lt;/span&gt; to be normal (asymptotically).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To meet out goal as a less expensive approach for resampling. Sample from &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho\)&lt;/span&gt; directly will be a less expensive approach.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Notes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Previously discuss a sphere-cloud approach, I modified it to the “wald-cloud”(approach 2) above, this is to consider the covariance of correlation under null.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
