&lt;section id="generating-uniform-samples-inside-confidence-region-of-rho_0" class="level1"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Generating uniform samples inside confidence region of &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho_0\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The goal is to generate &lt;strong&gt;uniform&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;random&lt;/strong&gt; samples &lt;strong&gt;within&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(C_{1-\alpha}(\rho_0)\)&lt;/span&gt; with guarantee: &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(C_{1-\alpha} = \{ \rho:  \ n(\hat{\rho} - \rho_0)^T\Psi^{-1}(\hat{\rho} - \rho_0)\ \leq \chi^{2}_{p, 1-\alpha} \}\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Modifying from the wald-cloud approach (matrix notation), a random uniform sample can be constructed by:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math display"&gt;\[
\hat\rho^{(b)} = \rho_0 + L(\sqrt{c} r^{\frac{1}{p}}Z)
\]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(c: \chi^2_{p, 1-\alpha}\)&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(r\)&lt;/span&gt; represent a random radius sample from &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(Unif(0,1)\)&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(Z\)&lt;/span&gt; represents the random unit direction, sample from &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(N(0,I_p)\)&lt;/span&gt; and rescale to have length (norm) 1 (i.e. &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(||Z|| = 1\)&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My intuition on this:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(c\)&lt;/span&gt; sets the &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(1-\alpha\)&lt;/span&gt; chi-square cut-off that determines the confidence ellipsoid boundary; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(r\)&lt;/span&gt; distributes a random radius (between (0,1)) uniformly in all &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(p\)&lt;/span&gt;-dimension within the ellipsoid; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(Z\)&lt;/span&gt; is the standard way to generate uniform random directions on a unit sphere. Geometrically, multiplying these three objects to &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(L\)&lt;/span&gt;is like rotating and scaling the ellipsoid.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="simulating-coverage" class="level1"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Simulating Coverage&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Definition of confidence region (CR):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math display"&gt;\[
Pr_\rho(\rho \in CR_{1-\alpha}(\hat\rho)) = 1-\alpha
\]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Frequentist interpretation of confidence region: Over repeated samples, the confidence region (which is random, depends on sample) contains the fixed true &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho\)&lt;/span&gt; with probability &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(1-\alpha\)&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The goal of simulation: check whether nominal and actual coverage of CR are the same, i.e., when &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\alpha = 0.05\)&lt;/span&gt;, do we get actual coverage of 95%?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Algorithm: Monte Carlo estimate of coverage of&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(CR_{1-\alpha}(\hat{\rho})\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Input:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho_0\)&lt;/span&gt;, sample size &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(n\)&lt;/span&gt;, significance level &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\alpha\)&lt;/span&gt;, number of replicates &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(B\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Output:&lt;/strong&gt; Coverage level &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(CR_{1-\alpha}(\hat\rho)\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(H \leftarrow 0\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;for&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(b\)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\leftarrow\)&lt;/span&gt; 1 to &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(B\)&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1. Sample &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho^{(b)}\)&lt;/span&gt; from &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(CR_{1-\alpha}(\hat\rho)\)&lt;/span&gt; [Algorithm X]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2. Compute covariance &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\Psi(\hat\rho^{(b)})\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3. Compute mahalanobis distance &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(T_b \leftarrow n(\rho_0-\hat{\rho}^{(b)})^{\top}[\hat\Psi(\hat\rho^{(b)})]^{-1}(\rho_0-\hat{\rho}^{(b)})\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;4. if &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(T_b \leq \chi^2_{p,1-\alpha}\)&lt;/span&gt;, then &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(H \leftarrow H+1\)&lt;/span&gt; ;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;return&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(H /B\)&lt;/span&gt; (actual coverage)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This simulation repeats over different &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho_0\)&lt;/span&gt;’s, which are uniformly sampled from the rlkj distribution by under different &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\eta\)&lt;/span&gt;. &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(n\)&lt;/span&gt; is set to &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(1e6\)&lt;/span&gt; to mirror aysmptotic-ness, &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\alpha = 0.05, 0.10\)&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(B=  200\)&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
